translations.text:
You, king of rivers, generous Tagus, who stole motion and warmth from the alabaster body you bathed, graceful, gallant, beautiful;


whether a natural musician among the shells you raised, or offering him the victorious branch from your native valley;


or, your currents freed from ice, giving him a mirror, I ask only that when he sees his image in you,


you show my tears beside his beauty, by which you grow rich: let his eyes at least behold them.

After always burning, never consumed, always weeping, never exhausted; after walking so far, never tiring, and always living, never dying;


after so much harm, not repenting; so much deceit, not learning; so many sorrows, not rejoicing, and so much pain, never laughing;


not losing myself in so many labyrinths, nor remembering after so much oblivion—what happy end can promise itself to me?


I shall be dead before I learn; I no longer mean to defend myself, but to be truly wretched.

Now the oak has more followers as embers than in leaf and fruit, and cloudy Orion stains the once-purple regions with mourning;


sluggish Arcturus and the Bears give little sun, and ice with gray command fashions waters into prisons;


today, when life is sought in warmth, thanks to ruling winter, a blind lover whom Love despises and Lisi forgets,


I bring my heart to her lovely ice, to see whether her snow will delight in my fire.
